# Natural-language hypothesis (data-grounded, 86 studies)

Across all three disciplines in this 86-study meta-replication dataset, original studies
report systematically larger effect sizes than the paired replications on the Fisher-z scale
(signed difference d = fiso − fisr; one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank p ≪ 0.001 in every
discipline). Quantitatively, percentiles on the signed tally are nearly identical across
domains: 88.2% of Social, 82.4% of Cognitive, and 83.3% of Economics studies show
fiso > fisr; the corresponding magnitude tally (|fiso| > |fisr|) is also 88.2% / 82.4% /
83.3% — i.e., the 18 replications in which the effect fully reverses sign on the r scale
(rr ≤ 0) do not flip the answer because, in this dataset, |rr| is consistently smaller
than ro even when rr is negative.

The domains **most clearly** in this pattern are **Social and Cognitive** (Psychology RPP,
n = 34 each), where the median Fisher-z gap d ≈ 0.23–0.25 (Wilcoxon one-sided p ≈ 10⁻⁶).
**Economics** (EERP, n = 18) shows a smaller but still clearly positive median gap
(d = 0.143, Wilcoxon one-sided p = 4 × 10⁻⁴). The pairwise Mann–Whitney comparisons on
d across the three disciplines are not significant after Benjamini–Hochberg correction
(Soc vs Cog p_BH = 0.58, Soc vs Econ p_BH = 0.58, Cog vs Econ p_BH = 0.71), and the
Kruskal–Wallis omnibus test is H = 1.25 with p = 0.54; therefore we do not have
statistical evidence that the magnitude of the original-replication gap differs across
disciplines, even though Cognitive leads and Economics trails by a few hundredths of a
Fisher-z unit.

Bottom line: originals exceed replications in every domain (Social, Cognitive, and
Economics), the typical Fisher-z gap is on the order of 0.23 (roughly 0.10–0.15 on the
correlation scale), and **no domain exhibits the reverse pattern**. The headline claim is
that *for all three disciplines studied*, effect-size estimates tend to be larger in
original studies than in their paired replications, with no significant between-domain
heterogeneity.
